\section{Related Work}

\subsection{Feature Trajectory}

Feature trajectory represents a novel and effective approach for modeling feature evolution over time. It has been successfully applied in various tasks, including motion estimation, object tracking, and action recognition. In this work, we introduce a new algorithm for predicting future features based on feature trajectories.

\subsection{Video Generation}

Video generation is a challenging task that has attracted significant research interest. Current methods generally fall into two categories: those based on pixel-level modeling and those based on feature-level modeling. Pixel-level methods typically employ deep learning techniques such as autoencoders, variational autoencoders, and generative adversarial networks. Conversely, feature-level approaches utilize deep features extracted from pre-trained models. Our proposed algorithm is specifically tailored for feature-level video generation.

\section{The Proposed Algorithm}

\subsection{Feature Trajectory Modeling}

A feature trajectory is defined as a three-dimensional tensor $\mathbf{F} \in \mathbb{R}^{T \times H \times W}$, where $T$ denotes the temporal dimension, and $H$ and $W$ represent the spatial dimensions of the feature map. Given a sequence of $T$ feature maps, the goal is to model their evolution.

\subsection{Feature Trajectory Learning}

To capture the evolution of feature maps, we propose a straightforward yet effective strategy. First, we extract a feature map from the initial video frame to serve as the trajectory's starting point. Subsequently, we compute the difference between each of the remaining $T-1$ feature maps and this initial map. These difference maps, alongside the original starting map, are concatenated to form the complete feature trajectory.

