\section{Introduction}

Reinforcement learning (RL) has become a dominant approach for motion planning and control, valued for its ability to learn control policies directly from state-action interactions. Among RL methods, actor-critic algorithms have gained significant traction due to their sample efficiency and capacity to generalize across diverse environments. Nevertheless, the substantial number of interactions required by actor-critic RL can be prohibitive for real-world applications. To mitigate this, we propose a curriculum learning framework that trains the actor-critic model on a sequence of incrementally difficult tasks, thereby enhancing sample efficiency.

